1
00:00:01,442 --> 00:00:03,832
Everyone shows a text-to-SQL demo.

2
00:00:04,292 --> 00:00:08,712
Very few people have to answer for the
number it gives at 9:00 in the morning.

3
00:00:09,811 --> 00:00:15,492
I run one of those systems, five hundred
data marts on our own servers, open

4
00:00:15,502 --> 00:00:17,802
models, no data leaves the building.

5
00:00:18,551 --> 00:00:24,242
I got tired of guessing which tricks
really help, so I tested all of them.

6
00:00:25,441 --> 00:00:28,061
Fifteen hundred queries,
three models families.

7
00:00:29,221 --> 00:00:30,781
I am Vladimir Beskrovanyy.

8
00:00:31,311 --> 00:00:36,471
I build AI systems for places
that cannot use a cloud API.

9
00:00:37,581 --> 00:00:39,111
This talk has two sources.

10
00:00:39,361 --> 00:00:41,062
One is my production system.

11
00:00:41,602 --> 00:00:42,581
You cannot check it.

12
00:00:43,451 --> 00:00:45,481
The other is a public benchmark.

13
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:48,021
The code is open, so you can.

14
00:00:48,981 --> 00:00:51,381
I will always say which one I'm using.

15
00:00:52,571 --> 00:00:56,251
In a demo, you have five
tables and one user.

16
00:00:56,661 --> 00:00:57,611
That user is you.

17
00:00:58,422 --> 00:01:02,351
In production, I have five hundred
marts and thirty million users.

18
00:01:02,831 --> 00:01:05,651
None of them can read SQL to check me.

19
00:01:06,741 --> 00:01:09,112
That is the whole difference.

20
00:01:09,831 --> 00:01:12,761
In a demo, a bad query
is a bad screenshot.

21
00:01:12,761 --> 00:01:15,541
In production, it is a wrong
number inside a real decision,

22
00:01:15,962 --> 00:01:18,051
and nobody sees it for a week.

23
00:01:18,822 --> 00:01:20,671
One warning before the numbers.

24
00:01:21,401 --> 00:01:22,771
There are two kinds.

25
00:01:23,401 --> 00:01:28,581
Production numbers come from
our own system, closer data

26
00:01:28,782 --> 00:01:30,712
or own definition of correct.

27
00:01:31,661 --> 00:01:33,721
Benchmark numbers come from BERT.

28
00:01:35,021 --> 00:01:36,091
They are public.

29
00:01:36,991 --> 00:01:38,991
You can run them against yourself.

30
00:01:39,672 --> 00:01:41,051
The two are not comparable.

31
00:01:41,532 --> 00:01:42,902
I will not compare with them.

32
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:47,332
Every time I say a number, I
will tell you which kind it is.

33
00:01:48,831 --> 00:01:50,741
So my questions was simple.

34
00:01:51,611 --> 00:01:55,711
Of all tricks people publish,
which ones work on small open

35
00:01:55,721 --> 00:01:57,501
models, and what do they cost?

36
00:01:58,501 --> 00:01:58,911
Bird, death.

37
00:01:59,261 --> 00:02:02,161
The full set, fifteen hundred
and thirty-four questions.

38
00:02:03,101 --> 00:02:04,341
The metric, execution accuracy.

39
00:02:04,701 --> 00:02:09,611
A query is correct only if
it returns the right result.

40
00:02:10,411 --> 00:02:14,451
Three families, Qwen two point
five, Coder at seven, fourteen,

41
00:02:14,501 --> 00:02:15,541
and thirty-two billion.

42
00:02:16,181 --> 00:02:19,341
CodeLlama at seven,
thirteen, and thirty-four.

43
00:02:19,981 --> 00:02:25,141
Llama three point one at eight billion
and three point three at seventy.

44
00:02:26,501 --> 00:02:32,011
Some prompt, some setting, some
test, all of our own servers.

45
00:02:32,481 --> 00:02:36,151
Every model runs the same
questions, so I compare them pair

46
00:02:36,151 --> 00:02:38,591
by pair with the McNemar test.

47
00:02:39,151 --> 00:02:41,651
I do not trust a two-point gap by eye.

48
00:02:42,651 --> 00:02:45,981
Add the size to it, half
points is still noise.

49
00:02:47,731 --> 00:02:52,991
First result, Qwen goes thirty-nine,
forty-seven, fifty as it grows.

50
00:02:53,811 --> 00:02:55,941
Seven to fourteen billion
gives eight points.

51
00:02:56,681 --> 00:02:58,541
Fourteen to thirty-two gives three.

52
00:02:59,241 --> 00:03:00,371
Growth stops fast.

53
00:03:01,041 --> 00:03:04,551
CodeLlama goes from twenty-one
to twenty-four and a half.

54
00:03:05,461 --> 00:03:06,961
It almost does not grow.

55
00:03:07,701 --> 00:03:11,451
At seven billion, the gap between
the two families is eighteen points,

56
00:03:12,151 --> 00:03:14,781
much bigger than any size effect.

57
00:03:16,321 --> 00:03:19,961
Now, CodeLlama is from twenty
twenty-three, and Qwen is

58
00:03:19,961 --> 00:03:21,221
from twenty twenty-four.

59
00:03:21,501 --> 00:03:22,221
Fair point.

60
00:03:22,711 --> 00:03:25,001
So I added a third family.

61
00:03:25,361 --> 00:03:28,401
Llama three point three at
seventy billion gets forty-nine.

62
00:03:29,001 --> 00:03:33,101
Qwen thirty-two gets
thirty-seven on the same setup.

63
00:03:33,371 --> 00:03:36,141
A modern model that is
not Qwen is strong too.

64
00:03:37,311 --> 00:03:40,401
Those two numbers come
from a compressed setup.

65
00:03:40,971 --> 00:03:46,021
Compare them to each other,
not to fifty as said before.

66
00:03:46,781 --> 00:03:48,761
So the lesson is not that Qwen wins.

67
00:03:49,231 --> 00:03:53,171
The lesson is that a new
model beats an old big one.

68
00:03:53,721 --> 00:03:56,611
Pick a recent family first, size second.

69
00:03:58,521 --> 00:03:59,401
Second result.

70
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:00,761
This is good news.

71
00:04:01,131 --> 00:04:03,084
One extra step, the model looks
at the own answer and fixes it.

72
00:04:03,084 --> 00:04:03,281
That is all.

73
00:04:04,281 --> 00:04:08,941
It helps every family plus three points
on Coder Llama Seven B, plus three

74
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:13,691
point seven on Llama Eight B, plus two
points on Coder Llama Thirty-Four B,

75
00:04:13,951 --> 00:04:20,051
plus one point two on Qwen Thirty-Two
B. The P values are on the slide.

76
00:04:20,841 --> 00:04:21,411
They're small.

77
00:04:22,341 --> 00:04:23,131
Look at a pattern.

78
00:04:23,361 --> 00:04:25,561
The stronger the model,
the smaller the gain.

79
00:04:26,241 --> 00:04:30,301
Llama Seventy B gains one point,
and that one is not significant.

80
00:04:30,721 --> 00:04:33,541
A fix step helps most when
there is a lot to fix.

81
00:04:34,891 --> 00:04:40,111
And this is what it fixes, a wrong join
key and a filter the model dropped.

82
00:04:40,611 --> 00:04:43,651
One extra pass, same
model, no fine-tuning.

83
00:04:44,131 --> 00:04:45,671
It never made things worse.

84
00:04:45,991 --> 00:04:49,281
If you take one thing from
this talk, take this one.

85
00:04:51,051 --> 00:04:51,471
Third result.

86
00:04:51,801 --> 00:04:54,611
This one took the most work
because I did not believe it.

87
00:04:55,131 --> 00:04:55,901
Schema linking.

88
00:04:56,261 --> 00:04:58,861
You cut the schema down
to the tables you need.

89
00:04:59,601 --> 00:05:01,501
It is in every tutorial.

90
00:05:01,891 --> 00:05:03,871
In my test, it makes things worse.

91
00:05:04,121 --> 00:05:09,821
Minus one point six on Qwen, minus one
point eight on Llama Eight B, minus

92
00:05:09,821 --> 00:05:14,961
three point seven on Llama Seventy
B. That last one is far from noise.

93
00:05:16,261 --> 00:05:20,991
People say my linker was too
simple, so I built a better one.

94
00:05:21,511 --> 00:05:25,901
An embedding model, top six tables,
ninety-six point five percent recall.

95
00:05:26,561 --> 00:05:28,371
The simple one had ninety-four.

96
00:05:29,131 --> 00:05:33,251
Better recall did not help,
even at ninety-six point five.

97
00:05:33,841 --> 00:05:35,831
Linking is no better than doing nothing.

98
00:05:36,541 --> 00:05:37,691
The reason is simple.

99
00:05:38,551 --> 00:05:40,031
On Bird, the schemas are small.

100
00:05:40,241 --> 00:05:45,731
The prompt was never the problem,
so cutting it cannot help, and

101
00:05:45,781 --> 00:05:47,951
it can drop a table you need.

102
00:05:48,141 --> 00:05:50,041
You take a risk and save nothing.

103
00:05:50,731 --> 00:05:55,951
Now I have to be careful because
this word meets my own systems.

104
00:05:57,121 --> 00:06:01,191
In production, I route
across five hundred mods.

105
00:06:01,561 --> 00:06:03,821
Every query goes through retrieval.

106
00:06:04,061 --> 00:06:07,271
So I am saying the
opposite of my own paper?

107
00:06:07,821 --> 00:06:12,481
No, and this difference matters
more than either result alone.

108
00:06:13,011 --> 00:06:16,191
On a small schema, retrieval is an option.

109
00:06:16,671 --> 00:06:19,961
You can measure it, and it
comes out negative, skip it.

110
00:06:20,731 --> 00:06:23,691
On five hundred mods,
retrieval is not an option.

111
00:06:24,271 --> 00:06:26,721
The schema does not fit in context window.

112
00:06:27,131 --> 00:06:30,261
Routing is the only way
the query works at all.

113
00:06:31,131 --> 00:06:32,701
Same name, two different jobs.

114
00:06:32,811 --> 00:06:37,231
I keep saying teams with small
schemas copy tricks that exist

115
00:06:37,271 --> 00:06:40,701
only because someone else had
a schema that did not fit.

116
00:06:41,441 --> 00:06:45,281
And to be fair, in the other
direction, I have not tested wide

117
00:06:45,301 --> 00:06:47,011
schema routing that carefully.

118
00:06:47,711 --> 00:06:53,727
That is next study, not a claim I make
today Third part of the architecture.

119
00:06:54,017 --> 00:06:55,417
Here, change source.

120
00:06:55,667 --> 00:06:58,127
Everything on the slide
is from production.

121
00:06:58,377 --> 00:06:59,817
I did not test any of it.

122
00:06:59,817 --> 00:07:02,677
The receipt or always blocked.

123
00:07:02,947 --> 00:07:04,107
Read-only access.

124
00:07:04,477 --> 00:07:06,097
Objects from a white list.

125
00:07:06,487 --> 00:07:09,767
A row limit on every
query, cheap and absolute.

126
00:07:10,357 --> 00:07:12,447
None of this stops the real problem.

127
00:07:12,997 --> 00:07:18,327
A query can pass all those check, be valid
SQL, and still answer the wrong questions.

128
00:07:18,637 --> 00:07:20,327
That is not a security problem.

129
00:07:20,657 --> 00:07:25,167
It is a mining problem, and I don't
know how to block it automatically.

130
00:07:25,887 --> 00:07:27,217
So person still checks.

131
00:07:27,587 --> 00:07:30,517
A person sees the numbers
before it becomes a decision.

132
00:07:30,807 --> 00:07:32,597
The model is allowed to be wrong.

133
00:07:33,107 --> 00:07:35,287
It is not allowed to be wrong silently.

134
00:07:36,737 --> 00:07:42,187
Fourth result, self-consistency generate
five answer and void, plus zero point one

135
00:07:42,227 --> 00:07:44,477
three points, not significant nothing.

136
00:07:44,977 --> 00:07:49,117
From the nothing you pay five times
more tokens, and your slow queries

137
00:07:49,307 --> 00:07:51,217
go from ten second for fifty.

138
00:07:51,497 --> 00:07:56,407
That is the difference between a tool
people use and a tool people stop opening.

139
00:07:57,637 --> 00:08:01,797
Compare that with self-correction
on Codellama thirty-four B. Twelve

140
00:08:01,817 --> 00:08:06,257
cent per thousand queries becomes
twenty-two cent, and the gain is real.

141
00:08:06,657 --> 00:08:08,537
I take that trade every time.

142
00:08:09,257 --> 00:08:13,647
I measure cost from real time
on the GPU at one dollar eighty

143
00:08:13,647 --> 00:08:15,517
per hour, not from tokens.

144
00:08:16,087 --> 00:08:18,377
The person paying the
bill pays for seconds.

145
00:08:18,807 --> 00:08:20,397
So my advice is very short.

146
00:08:20,777 --> 00:08:24,587
A recent family, a medium or
large size at self-correction.

147
00:08:24,907 --> 00:08:28,739
Skip the rest Now the limits.

148
00:08:29,119 --> 00:08:34,279
My best result on our own servers
is fifty points for person.

149
00:08:34,539 --> 00:08:37,279
Strong cloud models get
about fifty-seven here.

150
00:08:37,609 --> 00:08:40,679
System with extra training
reach seventy-one point eight

151
00:08:40,739 --> 00:08:42,369
at the same thirty-two billion.

152
00:08:42,899 --> 00:08:45,289
My setup is as simple as possible.

153
00:08:45,589 --> 00:08:47,809
No example, one call, no fine-tuning.

154
00:08:48,029 --> 00:08:52,129
That gap is the price of a
clean baseline, and it is real.

155
00:08:52,989 --> 00:08:56,939
BERT is one benchmark, and
it has no labeling errors.

156
00:08:57,259 --> 00:09:01,549
That is another reason I use
paired test and not raw difference.

157
00:09:02,049 --> 00:09:04,849
Some of my results run
on a compressed setup.

158
00:09:05,249 --> 00:09:07,279
Those support relative claims only.

159
00:09:07,309 --> 00:09:09,319
It is written in the paper.

160
00:09:09,759 --> 00:09:12,969
And I did not measure quantiles at all.

161
00:09:13,109 --> 00:09:16,669
Everything I know about that comes
from production, not from this study.

162
00:09:18,079 --> 00:09:19,689
Four things to take with you.

163
00:09:19,689 --> 00:09:22,409
Pick a seed family before
you pick a big model.

164
00:09:22,699 --> 00:09:24,589
The U matters more than the size.

165
00:09:24,939 --> 00:09:25,829
Add self-correction.

166
00:09:26,029 --> 00:09:29,579
It is cheap, it is work every
hour, and it never hurt.

167
00:09:29,919 --> 00:09:32,759
Do not cut your schema
because a tutorial says so.

168
00:09:33,439 --> 00:09:34,089
Measure it.

169
00:09:34,249 --> 00:09:35,759
On small schemas, it loses.

170
00:09:36,249 --> 00:09:38,359
And test your differences properly.

171
00:09:38,579 --> 00:09:41,609
Have the gains in the
field and inside the noise.

172
00:09:42,459 --> 00:09:46,949
Everything here's open, code settings
and the answer for every question,

173
00:09:47,429 --> 00:09:49,629
so you can check any claim yourself.

174
00:09:50,539 --> 00:09:52,499
Two things I would like someone to do.

175
00:09:52,779 --> 00:09:57,059
Run it on a wide schema and add
a coder model from the same year.

176
00:09:57,659 --> 00:09:59,459
Links are on the slide.

177
00:10:00,019 --> 00:10:00,519
Thank you.

